      "rev_n_11_bc60c6_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_bc60c6_0",
        "node_id": "n_11_bc60c6",
        "thought_text": "Consider using a graph traversal algorithm, such as Depth-First Search (DFS) or Breadth-First Search (BFS), to explore each room and determine if it's possible for the snake to return to its original position. This could provide a more accurate count of returnable rooms.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "6255932392076530271",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794411464.583958
      },
      "rev_n_12_661626_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_12_661626_0",
        "node_id": "n_12_661626",
        "thought_text": "Implement a more detailed state analysis for each room, considering the direction of the conveyor belts and their connectivity. This might involve creating a directed graph representation of the rooms and belts, then checking for cycles or paths that allow snakes to return to their starting rooms.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "6255932392076530271",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794411464.583969
      },
      "rev_n_13_83a7d4_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_13_83a7d4_0",
        "node_id": "n_13_83a7d4",
        "thought_text": "Instead of performing a DFS from each room, we can first categorize the rooms into groups based on their connectivity. Rooms that are connected by either clockwise or anticlockwise belts form different cycles or chains. We can then count the number of such groups to determine the number of returnable rooms.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4830363270505609927",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794411653.135755
      },
      "rev_n_14_7e9a5b_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_14_7e9a5b_0",
        "node_id": "n_14_7e9a5b",
        "thought_text": "We can use two arrays, one for tracking the forward connectivity (clockwise and off belts) and another for backward connectivity (anticlockwise and off belts). By iterating through these arrays, we can mark the start and end points of each connected component and count them as returnable rooms.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4830363270505609927",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794411653.135767
      },
      "rev_n_15_da7ab9_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_15_da7ab9_0",
        "node_id": "n_15_da7ab9",
        "thought_text": "Given the circular nature of the rooms, we can simulate the movement of snakes from each room and check if they can return to the same room. This can be done by maintaining a direction vector that updates based on the belt state and checking if the snake eventually returns to the starting position.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4830363270505609927",
